# Done-gate

**This file is yours.** `.claude/task-rules.md` is the portable task
lifecycle and deliberately does not decide what *done* means here. This file
does. `rasa.domain.code` seeds it with the engineering bar below; edit the
commands to match your `CLAUDE.md`, and tighten it as the project earns it.
`/sync` and `bin/init` never overwrite it.

## Where the gate runs

In this domain the stages line up with the pull request:

| stage | means here |
|---|---|
| `active/` | the branch is being worked; no PR, or a draft PR |
| `review/` | the PR is open and ready — **this gate has not passed yet** |
| `completed/` | every gate below passed **and the PR merged** |

A task moves `review/` → `completed/` with `.claude/bin/task pass <id> --by
<who>` only when **both** hold:

1. every acceptance criterion on the task is met and actually verified, and
2. **every gate below passes.**

If a gate fails, `.claude/bin/task reject <id> --note "<which gate, and why>"`
sends it back to `active/`. It does not sit in `review/` waiting to be forgiven,
and no gate is waived to finish faster: no disabled test, no `--no-verify`,
no "it passes locally".

## Where the evidence goes

Into the task's `## Completion report` (task-rules.md §11), before `pass`
moves it: the real test count and time, the build result, the PR number.
The `--note` on `pass` names the evidence in `tasks/history.tsv` — pass it
bare (`--note "build clean, 214 tests green, PR #88 merged"`); the tool
records it as `gate: …`, and `reject` as `gate not passed: …`.

---

## Gates

> Each gate names a command. The command strings live in `CLAUDE.md` — this
> file names *which* check must pass; `CLAUDE.md` says how to run it.

- [ ] **Build** — the project's build command succeeds with no new warnings.
- [ ] **Verification suite** — the project's test command passes **headless /
      non-interactive**, unfiltered, from a clean start. This is the gate, not
      the watched or focused variant. Record the count and the time.
- [ ] **Paired test** — a `change` or `defect` task ships with a test that
      fails without the change and passes with it, per the project's
      test-pairing convention in `CLAUDE.md`. An `upkeep`, `inquiry` or
      `record` task states why none applies.
- [ ] **Run check** — the project's run command boots and the affected flow
      works; no new console or log errors on the paths it touches.
- [ ] **In scope** — nothing outside the task's expected files changed, or the
      task was amended first and says why.
- [ ] **Merged** — the PR is merged to the integration branch (`main` unless
      `git-flow-rules.md` says otherwise). An open PR is `review/`, not done.

A docs-only change still runs the verification command before its PR — it
proves nothing else broke. If you skip it, say so in the report, with the
reason.

---

## Optional gates

Paste any of these above the line when the project is ready for them.

```markdown
- [ ] A second reviewer who did not write the change approved the PR.
- [ ] Coverage did not drop below the floor in `CLAUDE.md`.
- [ ] The migration ran forward and back on a copy of production data.
- [ ] The release note / CHANGELOG entry is written.
```

---

## Two things this file is not

**It is not the lifecycle.** How tasks move and what the frontmatter holds is
`.claude/task-rules.md`, the same in every RasaOS domain.

**It is not the engineering house rules.** Branching, PR shape, the change
ledger, hotfixes and postmortems are in `.claude/code-task-rules.md`. Keep
this file to the gate itself; a done-gate that grows into a style guide stops
being read at the moment it matters.
